xceedbwt/ - Xceed BWT decoder for ZIP method 18.

  xceedbwt.c/.h: MIT-0, Johnsonjh (from the Xtro-ZIP unzip60 fork).
    Callback pull/push API (`read_byte` 0..255/-1, `write_data`,
    optional CRC callback). `iz_xceedbwt_decode` (no separate CRC
    callback) still validates size + CRC internally via its own
    fallback, so the glue passes NULL and lets it check. Framing:
    ASCII size digit '1'..'9' (= digit * 100000 max block), 0x31
    block markers with bzip2-style payloads (no per-block magic or
    CRCs), 0x17 end marker + zero padding, then exact-consumption
    (one more read must return -1), size and CRC must match.
    `CZipTool::UnBwt` bounds reads by `csz` and passes ZIP size/CRC
    straight through; `szMode` is `bwt`.

Compiled in: xceedbwt.c only. Builds clean under VC6 (only
  <stddef.h> needed; no type collisions with 7zTypes/ppmd, so the
  glue lives in ZipTool.cpp directly, unlike PPMd).

Fixture trap (real, cost real debugging time): the Huffman length
  stream terminates EVERY symbol with a 0 bit, INCLUDING the first
  symbol of each group (exactly like bzip2's sendMTFValues). The
  hand-rolled Python encoder first omitted the k=0 terminator; the
  vendored decoder rejected every stream (length drift in group 1)
  while a same-bug independent reimplementation agreed with the
  encoder - which proves the FIXTURE was wrong, not the decoder
  (same lesson as DCL: never trust a hand encoder that only its
  own decoder accepts). Encoder core was also validated piecewise
  (BWT vs naive + LF-inversion, RLE1 vectors, Huffman round-trip +
  Kraft checks, length cap 20 with a proven-terminating repair).
  7-Zip 26.04 does NOT decode method 18, so no third-party cross-
  check exists; confidence rests on the strict decoder (markers,
  padding, exact consumption, CRC, size all enforced) plus
  byte-exact output on 6 diverse members. Verified 6/6 PASS
  CRC-exact (0 B .. 120 KB, incl. multi-block, RLE-heavy, empty).

AES-inner-BWT is refused at the inner gate: the decoder forces an
  internal CRC-vs-header check, but AE-2 headers carry no CRC (the
  auth tag is the anchor instead). Plain BWT is fully supported.
